A crossbar switch, comprising:
a number of rows and a number of columns crossing the rows at crossing points; 
a row input at each row configured to receive an input signal supplied to each row; 
controllable switch elements arranged at the crossing points via which an input signal supplied to a row is switched to a column crossing this row at this crossing point when a switching signal is supplied to the respective crossing point; 
a column output to which a switched input signal is delivered as an output signal at each column; 
amplifier elements of the controllable switch elements that comprise an amplifier input and an amplifier output, wherein the amplifier inputs are connected with the rows and the amplifier outputs are connected with the columns and are arranged equidistant along the rows, the amplifier elements comprising input capacitances and are wired such that they only consume electrical energy when the switching signal is supplied to the respective crossing point; and 
row wave resistors that are arranged at ends of the rows opposite the row inputs, a row voltage being applied to the rows via the row wave resistors; 

    wherein:
row conductors are fashioned such that they likewise have the resistance value of the row wave resistor, taking into account the input capacitances.

claims:
1. A method of controlling deflection of an optical beam at a beam steering element, comprising:
 I) deflecting the optical beam, said deflecting comprising the steps of:
 i. receiving switching instructions indicative of a target one of a plurality of potential transmit or receive elements; 
 ii. determining a first control signal associated with the target transmit or receive element, wherein said target transmit or receive element is associated with a target orientation relative to the beam steering element, and wherein determining the first control signal comprises:
 determining the target orientation associated with the target transmit or receive element; wherein determining the target direction of arrival or departure relative to the beam steering element comprises consulting a lookup table comprising entries respectively associated with the potential transmit or receive elements; 
 determining the first control signal on the basis of the target orientation associated with the target transmit or receive element; 

 iii. applying the first control signal to the beam steering element, thereby to cause deflection of the optical beam; 

 II) fine tuning an alignment of the deflected optical beam, wherein the target orientation associated with the target transmit or receive element is a target direction of arrival or departure relative to the beam steering element, and wherein said fine tuning comprises the steps of:
 i. applying a second control signal to the beam steering element to cause the deflected optical beam to undergo a degree of misalignment with respect to an optical receiver associated with the target transmit or receive element; 
 ii. determining a pointing error associated with the deflected optical beam, the pointing error being indicative of the degree of misalignment between the deflected optical beam and the optical receiver associated with the target transmit or receive element; 
 iii. causing a modification in the deflection of the optical beam by modifying the second control signal in accordance with the pointing error by:
 modifying the target direction of arrival or departure relative to the beam steering element; 
 determining the second control signal on the basis of the modified target direction of arrival or departure; 

 iv. monitoring a net modification to the target direction of arrival or departure relative to the beam steering element resulting from repeatedly modifying the target direction of arrival or departure relative to the beam steering element; 
 v. updating the entry in the lookup table associated with the target transmit or receive element to reflect the net modification to the target direction of arrival or departure relative to the beam steering element. 




     2. The method defined in  claim 1 , the beam steering element being capable of motion in a first direction in response to a first drive signal and also being capable of motion in a second direction independent of the first direction in response to a second drive signal, wherein determining the first control signal comprises determining the first and second drive signals for the beam steering element. 


     3. The method defined in  claim 1 , the lookup table being a first lookup table, wherein determining the first control signal comprises consulting a second lookup table comprising entries respectively associated with the potential transmit or receive elements. 


     4. The method defined in  claim 1 , the lookup table being a first lookup table, wherein determining the target orientation associated with the target transmit or receive element comprises consulting a second lookup table comprising entries respectively associated with the potential transmit or receive elements. 


     5. The method defined in  claim 4 , wherein determining the first control signal on the basis of the target orientation associated with the target transmit or receive element comprises consulting a third lookup table comprising entries indexed on the basis of potential target orientations. 


     6. The method defined in  claim 1 , wherein determining the first control signal on the basis of the target orientation associated with the target transmit or receive element comprises performing a computation of a pre-determined algebraic function of the target orientation. 


     7. The method defined in  claim 1 , wherein the target transmit or receive element is the target receive element, and the target orientation associated with the target receive element is the target direction of departure relative to the beam steering element. 


     8. The method defined in  claim 7 , further comprising modifying the second control signal in accordance with the pointing error until the pointing error falls to a pre-determined threshold. 


     9. The method defined in  claim 1 , wherein the target transmit or receive element is the target transmit element, and the target orientation associated with the target transmit element is the target direction of arrival relative to the beam steering element. 


     10. The method defined in  claim 9 , wherein the optical receiver associated with the target transmit element occupies a pre-determined area of detectability, and wherein applying the second control signal to the beam steering element causes the deflected optical beam to undergo a degree of misalignment with respect to the area of detectability. 


     11. The method defined in  claim 10 , wherein the pointing error associated with the deflected optical beam is indicative of the degree of misalignment between the deflected optical beam and the area of detectability. 


     12. The method defined in  claim 9 , further comprising modifying the second control signal in accordance with the pointing error until the pointing error falls to a pre-determined threshold. 


     13. Computer-readable media tangibly embodying a program of instructions executable by a computer to perform the method of  claim 1 . 


